  "abstract": "We prove an explicit lower bound for the quadratic signed-prime sum that is uniform in its observation cutoff. For a real primitive nonprincipal character of conductor $q$, Hasse-normalized inert-prime divisibility and split-prime falling-factorial rows bound the same square interpolation determinant. Strong induction at the strictly earlier scale $\\lfloor R/K\\rfloor$ cancels the observation-scale logarithm. We give the resulting constant $A_{K,q,X}$ for every integer $K\\ge16$ and $X\\ge\\max(400,16K,\\sqrt q)$, including the initial range and all rounding and ramification costs.\n\nFor $t=\\log q\\ge10$, a prescribed parameter rule yields $C_\\chi(x)>-t/2-2\\log t-6$ for every $x\\ge1$. Littlewood's transfer then gives $L(1,\\chi)>\\pi^2e^{-\\gamma}/[3(t+4\\log t+16)]$. The constant profile also implies $L'/L(1,\\chi)\\le A$, leading through classical Stechkin subtraction to uniform real-zero gaps, including $(1-\\beta)\\log q>0.80210$ for $q\\ge10^{10}$. The same subtraction gives a uniform gap of 1.06410 for complex zeros satisfying $0<|\\Im\\rho|\\log q\\le3/4$ in the same range. One fixed choice proves $h(-D)=1\\Rightarrow D<3000$, with $\\log3000=8.006367567650\\ldots$.\n\nGeneral factorial feedback and Hasse normalization are prior methods; the contribution is the complete bounded-profile induction and its consumers. Exact local controls and a reproducible interval certificate cover the numerical claims, with an analytic infinite-parameter tail. Prime-distribution estimates are imported. The class-number cutoff is weaker than existing determinant endpoints. A restricted replay of the published reduced-form computation in Paper4/v1 eliminates all but the known nine of the 911 fundamental discriminants below 3000. The classification is recovered without using it as an input; no new census or parameter optimality is claimed.",
